      <p class="lead"><strong>Contrast bath is a clinical term. Contrast therapy is the umbrella term. Sauna combo is a marketing and equipment term. All three describe the same hot-cold principle, just at different scope.</strong></p>

      <p>I get this question a lot. Are these three things different, or just marketing versions of the same idea? Short answer: they overlap, but each one means something specific depending on who's using it and why.</p>

      <h2 id="core-idea">The core idea they all share</h2>
      <p class="sub">One mechanism, three different scopes.</p>

      <p>Every version of this practice runs on the same mechanism. You alternate heat and cold to create vascular &ldquo;pumping,&rdquo; blunt pain signals, and shift mood and recovery. What changes is how narrow the term is, the context it's used in (medical vs. wellness vs. marketing), and what equipment gets implied.</p>

      <h2 id="contrast-bath">Contrast bath: the clinical term</h2>
      <p class="sub">A specific, protocol-driven form of contrast therapy.</p>

      <p>A contrast bath is a specific, protocol-driven form of contrast therapy. It comes out of physical therapy and rehab, not wellness culture.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Alternating hot and cold water immersion, usually two tubs or basins</li>
        <li>Often limb-focused (hand, ankle) rather than whole-body</li>
        <li>Hot water around 98&ndash;110&deg;F, cold around 50&ndash;60&deg;F in clinical protocols</li>
        <li>Timed cycles like 1 minute cold, 2&ndash;3 minutes hot, repeated for 10&ndash;20+ minutes</li>
        <li>Used for pain, swelling, stiff joints, and post-injury mobility</li>
      </ul>

      <p>If a physical therapist or doctor says &ldquo;contrast bath,&rdquo; they mean this specific setup. Two water temps, structured timing, one body part.</p>

      <h2 id="contrast-therapy">Contrast therapy: the umbrella term</h2>
      <p class="sub">The broad category that contains all of the above.</p>

      <p>Contrast therapy is the broad category. It's not limited to basins or tubs.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Sauna (traditional or infrared) into cold plunge or ice bath</li>
        <li>Hot tub into cold plunge</li>
        <li>Steam room into cold shower</li>
        <li>Classic contrast bath setups</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Clinical research defines it as repeated thermotherapy (heat) and cryotherapy (cold), with the exact method and timing varying by protocol. You'll see it used in sports recovery, spa and wellness studios, and home hot tub plus cold plunge setups. When someone says &ldquo;I'm doing contrast therapy,&rdquo; they usually mean any hot-cold routine, not specifically a bath.</p>

      <h2 id="sauna-combo">Sauna combo: the equipment term</h2>
      <p class="sub">Not a clinical term at all &mdash; it's a product category.</p>

      <p>Sauna combo isn't a clinical term at all. It's a product category.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Usually means a paired sauna plus cold plunge or ice bath, sold or marketed together</li>
        <li>Shows up as &ldquo;sauna and cold plunge combo,&rdquo; &ldquo;sauna combo kit,&rdquo; or &ldquo;outdoor sauna and cold plunge combo unit&rdquo;</li>
        <li>Describes the hardware, not the protocol</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The practice you actually do in a sauna combo is contrast therapy. Specifically, sauna-based contrast therapy. You can do contrast therapy without a branded combo unit (a gym sauna plus a cold shower at home counts), and you can own a combo unit and still not alternate properly.</p>

      <h2 id="comparison">Quick comparison</h2>
      <p class="sub">Three terms, three different scopes.</p>

      <div class="tbl-wrap">
        <table class="tbl-clean">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it describes</th><th>Where you'll hear it</th></tr>
          </thead>
