            <tr><td>Contrast bath</td><td>Water-only hot-cold immersion, protocol-driven, often limb-focused</td><td>Physical therapy, rehab clinics</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Contrast therapy</td><td>Any structured hot-cold alternation</td><td>Sports recovery, wellness studios, research</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sauna combo</td><td>Bundled sauna and cold plunge hardware</td><td>Product pages, buying guides, studio menus</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="decide">How to decide what you're actually looking for</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Recovering from an injury, targeting one joint or limb:</strong> you want contrast bath protocols, ideally under a PT's guidance</li>
        <li><strong>General whole-body recovery or a mental health ritual, at home or at a studio:</strong> you're looking at contrast therapy broadly &mdash; sauna plus cold plunge is the most common form</li>
        <li><strong>Planning a home setup and want minimal DIY:</strong> a sauna combo kit is built for you</li>
        <li><strong>Comfortable with DIY and want flexibility or lower cost:</strong> buy the sauna and the chiller separately &mdash; you're still doing contrast therapy, just without the branded package</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Is a sauna combo the same as contrast therapy?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>No. A sauna combo is the equipment. Contrast therapy is what you do with it. You could own a sauna combo and never alternate hot and cold properly, and you could do contrast therapy with a gym sauna and a cold shower with no combo unit involved.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Can I do a contrast bath at home?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>Yes, with two basins or tubs and a thermometer, though most people doing this outside a clinical setting are closer to general contrast therapy than a true clinical contrast bath protocol. If you're managing a specific injury, check with a physical therapist before designing your own timing and temperatures.</p></div>
      </details>

      <p>If you want the full picture on how hot-cold cycling works and the two distinct protocols that share the name, start with our <a href="/learn/contrast-therapy-101">Contrast Therapy 101 guide</a>.</p>

